.TH UMOUNT-WINDOWS 8 "September 2026" "qcom-ath-board-update" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
umount-windows \- unmount a Windows volume mounted by mount-windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B umount-windows
.RI [ options ] " " [ target ]
.br
.B umount-windows
.B --all
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B umount-windows
releases a Windows volume previously mounted by
.BR mount-windows (8),
including any BitLocker
.B dislocker
mount it depends on, and removes the temporary directories it created.
.PP
.I target
is either the mountpoint printed by
.B mount-windows
or the source block device that was mounted.
.B umount-windows
only acts on mounts it can find in its own state; it refuses targets it did
not create.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --all
Unmount every Windows volume currently tracked by
.BR mount-windows ,
instead of a single target.
.TP
.B --force
Force the unmount, as with
.BR "umount -f" .
.TP
.B --lazy
Detach the mount lazily, as with
.BR "umount -l" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /run/mount-windows
State tracking the mounts created by
.BR mount-windows (8),
consulted and updated by this tool.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns zero on success and non-zero if the target is not a mount tracked by
.BR mount-windows ,
if the unmount fails, or if the caller is not root.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount-windows (8),
.BR qcom-ath-board-update (8),
.BR umount (8)
